The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
lock_sock(sk);
if (!sk->sk_net_refcnt) {
    sk_net_refcnt_upgrade(sk);
}
</text>
Reason:
`lock_sock(sk)` is called first, acquiring the socket lock. Inside that critical section, `sk_net_refcnt_upgrade(sk)` is called, which performs a GFP_KERNEL memory allocation via `get_net_track()->ref_tracker_alloc()`. This allocation can trigger `fs_reclaim`, creating a circular dependency: the socket lock is held while `fs_reclaim` may need to wait for the same socket lock (e.g., through memory reclaim paths), leading to a deadlock.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
if (!sk->sk_net_refcnt) {
    if (!maybe_get_net(net))
        return false;
    sk_net_refcnt_upgrade(sk);
    put_net(net);
}
lock_sock(sk);
</text>
Reason:
The `sk_net_refcnt_upgrade()` call is moved outside the `lock_sock()` critical section, so it runs before acquiring the socket lock. This eliminates the circular dependency because the GFP_KERNEL allocation now occurs without holding the socket lock. The socket lock is acquired later only for the buffer size modifications, which do not trigger memory reclaim. The `release_sock(sk)` that was previously inside the if block is also removed because the lock is no longer held at that point.

